Q: Is there a way to use an external file to get a list of values and use those values in an Excel formula? I am trying to create a formula in Excel where it reads a list of values from an external text file and uses those values in a formula.
This file will be updated frequently, so I need a way to make sure my formula in Excel is always pulling the most recent values from the file.
Is this something that can be done?
I've seen references to the use of an ImportXML, ImportData or the Text to Columns function, but none that seem to be able to use a range of values from an external text file.
If it is not possible using Excel's built in functions, I am looking for workarounds or a way to refresh the data automatically each time this spreadsheet is opened.
Would it be a better idea to create a separate Excel file that updates the list of values each time and then link it by reference or is there a more elegant solution?
Any suggestions would be greatly appreciated.

A: Unfortunately, Excel as a software alone can not read external text file. But it can read CSV files.
Here are steps you can perform:


*

*Write out the numbers in the text file, separating each number by a comma (CSV format). You should look at the CSV file manually before you import it to Excel. There cannot be any blanks in the numbers, if they are integers. If they have a decimal, then ensure there are no commas after the decimal. So for example, your values like 123, 234, 456 will be read incorrectly as two separate values because of commas, so make sure they are all one decimal like 123.0, 234.5, 456.2 etc.

*Once you've written out your numbers in CSV format, you can import it to Excel. You do this by going to Data > Get External Data > From Text or Excel > from a Web Page > From Other Sources > From CSV and upload your file.

*After that, you can simply reference your data from the imported area in your excel spreadsheet.


The drawback here, is if your file changes and you have to update, you would have to manually do it again. That being said, a better, permanent solution for your needs would be to create a small script or macro that could read your text file and update the values in Excel directly. Excel VBA will allow you to do that.
Another idea would be to use an online tool like Google Sheets available for read an external text file and display it in a website, and reference it in excel. Or you can use formulas with Web Query (Data > Web Query > Manually enter your URL/file) to retrieve the data from your remote text file.

A: Unfortunately, Excel can't directly read from external text files or files outside Excel, but we can make use of Excel's data model and Power Query to deal with this problem in the short term.
This will involve setting up a connection to your text files and setting Excel to query the data whenever you open the workbook. 
Here is a step by step guide on how to do it:


*

*Click on "Data" > "Get Data" > "From Text" > "From File"

*Find and select your Text File

*You may have to adjust "Data format settings" to make sure Excel can read your data correctly.

*Under "Navigation" settings, select the delimiter (most likely comma, space, or Tab)  

*Click "Load"

*You will then be taken to Power Query screen, where you can clean and transform your external text data. Save & Load it back to the Excel.


Now when you open your workbook, Power Query will automatically query your external data from your text file and refresh the worksheet.
Note: To perform automatic refresh each time you open the workbook, you may have to place a macro that performs the above process automatically in the Workbook_Open event. 
However, if your data in the text file frequently changes, using Excel VBA to update it from the file could be a more reliable automated solution.
Edit: I should mention, if you have large data and performance is an issue, make sure you have the latest version of Power Query.

